WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:32.980
[Background music]

00:00:49.220 --> 00:01:00.840
[An ephemeral image of clouds and a horse and buggy approaching from the left come into view.]

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:08.120
[Dr. Lowell:] Howdy, folks, remember me? John Lowell, family doctor.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.100
Time was when I took pretty good care of the people hereabouts.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:18.660
...was with them when they were born and when they died, tried to make the years in between

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:25.660
just as healthy and happy as I could. But
I don't get around here very much nowadays.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:32.090
[Ghostly figure of Dr. Lowell drives along
in horse-drawn carriage and stops in front of a home,

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:39.820
then gets out and approaches the front door.]

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:45.770
I guess maybe you're wondering what I'm doing
here now. Well, it's like this, I've been

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:51.399
wanting to drop in on Plainville again, visit
some of the folks, see how the old world's

00:01:51.399 --> 00:01:54.649
getting along.

00:01:54.649 --> 00:01:57.640
[Music]

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:03.120
Let's look in on the Jones family, see how
they're doing.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.620
[A family sits around the table preparing to have breakfast.]

00:02:07.739 --> 00:02:14.739
You know them, don't you? They're your neighbors,
could be yourselves. 

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:23.230
This is Henry Jones. Henry's a skilled mechanic down at the McGregor
Tool and Die Works.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:28.320
Looks like he's reading something that bothers him.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:35.280
Here's Sally and her baby. Sally was married to Henry's oldest son, David. 

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:42.290
He was with MacArthur in the Pacific, one of the expendables. He won't be coming back.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:50.480
And here's my old friend Deborah Jones, Henry's
mother. I knew Debbie when she was in pigtails.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:56.000
Still got that saucy gleam in her eye though,
and that stubborn chin, forever talking about

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.820
the good old days. Just won't give in to admitting how much better things are today.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:09.070
[Radio announcer:] Hello, hello, hello, this
is Jolly Charlie again with a brand new tune,

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:10.290
Speak of Moonlight.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.350
[Henry:] Turn that thing off.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:17.970
[Granny:] Now Henry Jones, we let you read your newspaper in peace, you let us enjoy our radio.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.760
[Henry:] All right, all right, mother, anything
to keep the peace. But it hurts.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:28.160
[Granny:] That was my favorite announcer and
he always plays the records I like.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.750
[Sally:] [Yes, Granny.]

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:33.360
[Grace:] Hi everybody.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:34.400
[Henry:] Hello honey.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.880
[Sally:] Where are you working today, Grace?

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:40.020
[Grace:] At the high school, Sally. Dr. Johnson's
finishing up his student's physical examination.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:41.600
Bill's going to help too.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.880
[Granny:] That accounts for your high spirit.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.320
[Grace:] What'd you say, Granny?

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.000
[Granny:] Oh, shut that thing off. Nobody
can hear me.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:53.310
[Granny:] I said you're as merry as a cricket
because Bill McGregor's going to be with you today.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:59.390
[Mother:] Now Granny, you know Bill has nothing to do with it. Gracie is there because she loves her work.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.750
[Granny:] Oh yes, I suppose it's just one
of those things, Gracie taking a job in a

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.400
hospital where Bill's an intern.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.650
[Grace:] You know very well I like my job,
Granny. It's swell being a part of such wonderful work.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:15.880
And those health records I've been keeping
up with school kids' exams...that's health

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.080
in the bank, Granny. They pay real dividends.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:22.320
[Granny:] Well, I do declare. What are the
examinations for? 

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.140
Is there some kind of an epidemic in town?

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.000
[Laughter]

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:30.240
[Grace:] Goodness no, we don't wait for epidemics,
Granny, we try to prevent them.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.270
[Granny:] Well, in my day we didn't go to
a doctor until we were really sick.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:38.349
[Grace:] That's just the point; you can't
tell whether or not you're really sick. 

00:04:38.349 --> 00:04:40.680
That's up to the doctor.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:47.680
[Henry:] That girl has a lot of sense, mother. You shouldn't belittle her interest in her work.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:56.800
[Mother:] Now where is that boy? Late again. Peter, Peter.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.200
[Peter:] Okay, okay, I'll make it.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:03.290
[Sally:] We have a date this afternoon, haven't
we, Dickey boy? We're going to go to the clinic

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:09.960
and see that everything's all right, every
little finger and toe and eye and ear

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.250
and that new tooth.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:15.570
[Granny:] I think it's all stuff and nonsense,
taking the baby to the clinic every month.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.900
If he were my baby I'd never take him there
at all, being pawed over and bounced around

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.690
by a lot of doctors and nurses and all sorts
of people.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.440
[Mother:] Why don't you go down to the clinic
and see for yourself what it's like, Granny?

00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:33.470
[Granny:] Who me? Deborah Jones go to a clinic?

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:36.460
[Peter:] Watch it, Granny, you'll get high
blood pressure.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.120
[Granny:] High blood pressure, fiddlesticks.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:41.740
[Henry:] I think Sally's smart to watch out
for the baby's health like she does.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:48.740
It'd be a good thing if we all did it. I was just reading here, 160,000 people die of cancer

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.600
every year.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:58.600
Some of these deaths are the result of people going to quacks for treatment, 

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:02.840
some because they don't go to an accredited doctor until
too late.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:09.540
But, cancer can be cured if it's treated in
time and by the approved methods of 

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.949
surgery, x-ray, and radium.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:18.120
And it says that tuberculosis and a lot of
other diseases can be cured if they're discovered

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:21.780
early enough. People certainly ought to go
to the doctor.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.760
[Sally:] Instead of doctoring themselves.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.760
[Granny:] We didn't go looking for trouble
in my day.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:31.350
[Henry:] No mother, you didn't. And that's
why so many died before their time.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:37.210
[Granny:] I still say when it comes time for
a person to die, they die. 

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.240
There's just nothing anybody can do about it.

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:40.520
[Henry:] Oh well.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:46.260
[Dr. Lowell:] I wouldn't say that, Deborah.
If I'd known what doctors know now, I might

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:50.580
have been able to save your baby, the one
that died of diphtheria.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:56.600
Nowadays, diphtheria toxoid is saving thousands
of lives every year.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:01.900
Deborah Jones, stop gulping those pills and listen to me.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:07.500
Sally believes in prevention and deep down
in your heart you know she's right, only you're

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.790
too darn stubborn to admit it.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:13.940
[Granny:] I'm not stubborn.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:18.400
[Henry:] Well nobody said you were, mother.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.280
[Mother:] What's the matter, Granny?

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:27.400
[Granny:] Why, nothing's the matter. What
should be the matter? Mind your baby, Sally.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.180
[Henry:] Well, I've got to be going.

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:30.540
[Peter:] Goodbye Pop.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:31.420
[Granny:] Goodbye son.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:32.980
[Mother:] Goodbye Henry.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:34.420
[Henry:] Goodbye. Goodbye mother.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.430
[Mother:] Goodbye dear.

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:42.820
[Granny:] If you go to food like that, young
man, you'll need one of my digestion pills.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:47.040
[Peter:] No I won't, Granny, with a breakfast
like this under my belt, nothing will hurt me.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:51.240
[Granny:] Don't be impudent, Peter, and don't
talk with your mouth full.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.940
[Grace:] Don't forget to show up for your
physical at school this morning 

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:55.920
and get your chest x-ray report.

00:07:55.930 --> 00:08:01.830
[Peter:] Okay, okay. Hey sis, did you see
that x-ray report? Was it all right?

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:05.080
[Mother:] Why Peter, I'm so glad to see you
take an interest in your health.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:09.630
[Peter:] I don't care anything about my health.
I just want to be okay for football.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:13.790
[Grace:] Being okay for football isn't being
healthy, oh certainly not.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:20.140
[Phone ringing.] Oh, that's Bill. I'll get it.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:26.600
Hello? Yes darling, I'm just leaving now.
Yes, I'll be right over.

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:31.180
[Bill:] Mother and I are a bit worried about
dad. I want to stop off at the plant and see how he's feeling.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:37.900
[Grace:] Oh, all right dear, bye bye.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:46.000
Bill's worried about his father. He's going
to get the plant doctor

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:47.960
 to sell him the idea of a checkup.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:49.560
[Mother:] I hope it's nothing serious.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:54.360
[Grace:] I hope not. I'll ask Bill about it
when I see him. I'll have to run along.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.840
[Peter:] Me too. Goodbye mom. Goodbye gang.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:58.860
[Mother:] Goodbye, son.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:00.140
[Grace:] Goodbye, Peter.

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:00.740
[Granny:] Goodbye dear.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.200
[Grace:] Oh Sally, I left the health pamphlet
that you wanted over there.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.330
[Sally:] Oh swell. Have a good day, Grace.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.860
[Grace:] Thanks. Bye everybody.

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:09.740
[Mother:] Well I'll clear away.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.960
[Sally:] Oh, I'll help.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:38.100
[Mother and Sally begin clearing the table. Granny waits until they leave the room, then gets up, locates a document on the sideboard, and slips it into her pocket.]

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:43.420
[Sally:] You wouldn't want to come to the
clinic with Dickey and me today, would you?

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:48.940
[Granny:] Now Sally, don't start that again.
By the way, Sally, has your baby had its 

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:51.800
diphtheria shot or whatever you call it?

00:09:51.809 --> 00:09:55.139
[Sally:] Why, of course he has. What made you ask that?

00:09:55.139 --> 00:09:59.759
[Granny:] I was just remembering. Well, never
mind...

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:05.119
[Sally:] Maybe if you'd read about what's
going on, that pamphlet that Grace left me.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:10.480
Well, what's become of it?

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:12.500
[Granny:] Guess I'll go over to see Mrs. Allen.

00:10:12.509 --> 00:10:19.509
[Sally:] That's funny, I'm sure she left it
here. I wonder who picked it up?

00:10:19.769 --> 00:10:25.209
[Granny:] Oh, I suppose anybody is liable
to pick up things that are lying around.

00:10:25.209 --> 00:10:32.209
I guess that's why folks leave them lying about, just to tempt people. Stubborn, who's stubborn?

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:37.180
High blood pressure, ha [inaudible].

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:42.420
[Sally:] Now what can be the matter with Granny?

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:47.660
[Dr. Lowell:] I'll tell you. Tthe matter with
Granny is that the old hard shell seems to

00:10:47.999 --> 00:10:52.279
be softening up a bit. She's always been so sure of herself, 

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:54.779
but it isn't her pills that keep her going.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:01.960
It's the walk she takes, the foods she eats,
the catnap she has after lunch.

00:11:07.239 --> 00:11:13.319
Too bad I can't give Granny A for attitude.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:23.920
Now for a look at the younger generation.
Here's a mighty fine school health service.

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:28.420
These Plainville doctors are certainly doing
a grand job.

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:33.720
Periodic checkups like these give them a chance
to prevent illness 

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:37.040
instead of half a chance to cure it.

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:42.140
Bright youngster this, but lately she's been
falling behind in her work. 

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:47.360
Now the mystery is solved. Jane needs glasses.

00:11:47.369 --> 00:11:54.369
Tommy once had rheumatic fever, growing pains
some folks call it, but the wise ones, like

00:11:54.610 --> 00:12:01.610
Tommy's mother, sent him to the doctor and that's why Tommy's all right now.

00:12:02.059 --> 00:12:07.259
Bill McGregor is responsible for a mighty
important part of the checkup.

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:14.120
Teeth certainly get a lot of care these days
and that's as it should be.

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:21.120
And our young friend, Peter Jones, wonder
if he'll make out all right?

00:12:21.619 --> 00:12:23.379
[Doctor:] Everything's fine, Pete.

00:12:23.379 --> 00:12:25.660
[Peter:] And the chest x-ray too?

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:30.129
[Doctor:] I'm glad to see you weren't worried
about it. Some of the other boys who had a

00:12:30.129 --> 00:12:32.909
positive tuberculin test got all excited.

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:37.000
[Peter:] Why should I get excited? You told
me that didn't mean I had TB, just that I

00:12:37.009 --> 00:12:38.720
needed a chest x-ray to make sure.

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:42.759
[Doctor:] Good boy, Pete. I wish everyone
would listen as carefully and get the facts right.

00:12:43.009 --> 00:12:45.259
[Peter:] So I can go out for the team?

00:12:45.259 --> 00:12:47.399
[Doctor:] You certainly can.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:54.400
[Dr. Lowell:] Too bad Grandpa Jones didn't
live to see Peter make the team.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:13:02.640
But Grandpa died of TB, didn't even know he had it until it was too late.

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:18.880
[Music, then background noise in a plant.]

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:23.540
[Bill:] Annette O'Brian, Joe Benton and Sam Carsey as committee members. As chairman

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:29.400
of our new health and safety committee you
have elected Henry Jones.

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:37.180
All of you men get behind your committee and let's put this health program over with a bang.

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:40.000
[Dr. Stewart:] How are you, Jones?

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:42.980
[Henry:] How are you, doctor?

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:49.780
[Dr. Stewart:] It's a real job and I know
you can handle it. Let's go in and see the big boss.

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:53.180
We have a date to talk things over
and I'd like you to come along and give us your ideas.

00:13:54.199 --> 00:14:00.239
[Henry:] Fine. Just a minute. Grant, take
over, will you? 

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:02.460
You know I think we've got something, doctor.

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:05.279
[Dr. Stewart:] Glad you feel that way.

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:10.119
[Henry:] Doctor, looks like my lunchbox.

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:17.120
[Dr. Stewart:] That's vitamin glamour my boy. [Laughter] 

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:18.860
Go right in, Jones.

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:19.980
[Henry:] Thank you, doctor.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:25.140
[Dr. Stewart:] Our new health committee chairman,
Mr. McGregor.

00:14:25.149 --> 00:14:28.209
[McGregor:] Yes, I just heard. Congratulations,
Jones.

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:30.520
[Henry:] Thank you, Mr. McGregor. I'll do
the best I can.

00:14:30.529 --> 00:14:32.499
[McGregor:] Won't you sit down, gentlemen?

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:37.380
[Dr. Stewart:] The health department has notified
me they can move in their equipment for a

00:14:37.389 --> 00:14:40.679
chest x-ray next week. How about it Mr. McGregor?

00:14:40.679 --> 00:14:45.959
[McGregor:] Fine, I think a chest x-ray is
a safety device that every man needs.

00:14:45.959 --> 00:14:51.459
Our plant has a record for working conditions, less absenteeism from illness and injury than most.

00:14:51.859 --> 00:14:56.209
[Henry:] In these mass x-rays, Dr. Stewart,
does everybody get to know the other fellow's business?

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:58.970
The boys wouldn't like that.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:02.460
[Dr. Stewart:] No, the reports are confidential, like all the medical examinations in this plant.

00:15:02.649 --> 00:15:07.299
[McGregor:] Another thing, the x-rays are
free to the men and made on company time too.

00:15:07.299 --> 00:15:12.809
[Henry:] Well, in that case I'm sure the boys will be 100 percent for it. I wonder if you could

00:15:12.809 --> 00:15:16.499
give us some sort of health pamphlets and
things like that that we could hand around,

00:15:16.499 --> 00:15:17.649
[McGregor:] How about it?

00:15:17.649 --> 00:15:22.459
[Dr. Stewart:] Yes, sure. We can get plenty
of good information free, like those leaflets

00:15:22.459 --> 00:15:27.569
from the tuberculosis association. You know
they're working very closely every step of

00:15:27.569 --> 00:15:34.569
the way with the health department. We can get movies too, and transcriptions for the loudspeaker.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.600
[McGregor:] Any other ideas?

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:42.080
[Dr. Stewart:] I'd like an occasional half
hour on the company's time for a health talk to the men.

00:15:42.269 --> 00:15:47.239
[McGregor:] Well, I feel sure the board will
grant that request. I'll take it up with them.

00:15:47.239 --> 00:15:49.160
Another thing, I...

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:54.299
[McGregor faints onto his desk. Dramatic
music plays.]

00:15:55.780 --> 00:15:58.160
[Dr. Stewart:] Better call an ambulance, Jones.

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:03.060
[Henry:] Hello operator, call an ambulance
quickly, yes.

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:08.900
[Bill:] Don't worry, Dad, you're going to
be all right.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:12.400
[McGregor:] They tell me I have coronary heart trouble. What does that mean?

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:18.960
[Dr. Stewart:] Coronary heart trouble? 

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:26.120
Well, without getting too technical it simply means that your coronary arteries have hardened up a bit.

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:33.700
When that happens the heart muscle gets less blood, not enough food and oxygen.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.860
[McGregor:] I thought that happened to old
men. I'm only 48.

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:40.980
[Dr. Stewart:] You're in the prime of life.
But this kind of heart trouble hits hardest

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:43.260
at men around your age.

00:16:43.269 --> 00:16:46.369
[McGregor:] Am I all washed up, doctor?

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:50.620
[Dr. Stewart:] No, of course not. But you're
going to have to stay right where you are

00:16:50.629 --> 00:16:56.649
for a while and obey orders. It's not going
to be quick and it's not going to be easy

00:16:56.649 --> 00:17:03.809
to get you back on your feet again. You've
got to get your mind off the plant and stop worrying.

00:17:04.360 --> 00:17:08.620
[Bill:] Cheer up, young fellow. You've got
all the specialists in the hospital and your

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:11.280
own son to look after you.

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:15.440
[Dr. Stewart:] Remember now, take it easy.

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:25.500
[Dr. Lowell:] Yes, Bill, your dad's a pretty
sick man, but we know he's not fighting alone

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:32.800
and he knows it, too. And you've got this
up-to-date hospital with its modern blood bank,

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:40.080
its lifesaving medicine, new as today's
arrivals, 

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:47.000
physical therapy at its latest and best.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:51.080
All important laboratory service.....

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:05.920
X-ray that looks inside the body and takes
the guesswork out of diagnosis,

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:17.200
skilled hands and brains, disciplined teamwork, ready for any emergency.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.320
[Grace:] How's your father, Bill?

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:29.900
[Bill:] It's going to be a long hard pull,
Gracie.

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:36.280
[Grace:] I'm sure he'll be all right. He's
always been so healthy. I don't know much

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:42.320
about medicine, Bill, and please don't think
I'm just being a little dumb, Bill.

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:47.210
[Bill:] You're being just swell, Gracie. It
means a lot having you here with me now.

00:18:47.320 --> 00:18:49.800
[Grace:] You know I love you, Bill. 

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:55.100
I'm glad I can be here close to you and your father when you need me.

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:57.900
[Bill:] So am I, honey.

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:03.620
[Grace:] Why that's Granny going into the
clinic with Sally and the baby.

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:12.100
[Sally:] This is Mrs. Deborah Jones, nurse,
Dickey's great-grandmother. This is her very

00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:18.250
first visit to the clinic and I persuaded
her to come. It was a tough fight but we made it.

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:23.760
[Nurse:] Dr. Johnson is ready to see you now, Mrs. Jones, you may go in.

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:28.000
[Sally:] Granny, you mean you actually made
an appointment here before we even talked

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:29.310
about coming?

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:32.340
[Granny:] You don't think a slip of a girl
like you could persuade me to do something

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:37.230
I hadn't a mind to do? I persuaded myself.

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:41.420
Or maybe it was that voice that's been whispering to me.

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:43.840
[Sally:] What voice?

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:47.900
[Granny:] You wouldn't know. It was someone
long before your time.

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:52.570
[Sally:] What are you going to have done?
What's the matter, Granny?

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:57.570
[Granny:] You're always asking what's the
matter? Nothing's the matter. I'm going to

00:19:57.570 --> 00:20:04.570
have a general checkup. I guess I can have
one if everybody else in the town is having one.

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:08.840
I'm not as stubborn as you think.

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:13.250
[Sally:] But I never said you were, Granny.

00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:20.060
[Dr. Lowell:] Yes, it's a wonderful thing,
this clinic. No wonder the people of Plainville

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:28.410
are using it more and more, including Granny.
But Sally knew about it all along.

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:34.410
Dickey is the healthy proof of that.

00:20:54.480 --> 00:21:00.860
[Sally, reading questionnaire:] Do you take your baby regularly to the physician or the well-baby clinic?

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:01.540
Yes.

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:08.540
Has every member of your household had a recent
physical examination including a chest x-ray?

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:15.230
Yes. Even Granny.

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:23.180
Dickey is one baby well-protected against
disease. 

00:21:23.260 --> 00:21:28.420
He's going to be healthy, wealthy, and wise.

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:31.120
[Peter:] How're you doing, Granny?

00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:37.140
[Granny:] They tell me I'm doing all right,
Peter. But I always knew that. 

00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:44.640
You won't catch me winding up with too little, too late. It can't happen to me.

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:55.140
I'm doing all right.

00:21:59.120 --> 00:22:04.500
[Dr. Lowell:] That you are, Deborah. When
an old diehard like you sees the light,

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:12.220
it proves nobody's ever too old to learn.

00:22:14.150 --> 00:22:20.090
You know, doctors have found out a lot of
new things since my day, yes siree.

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:24.700
Nowadays they can prevent illness. Of course, they can't do it alone.

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:31.700
They must have your help. That's why the ones who give me hope are the young folks: 

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:39.220
Grace and Bill, Sally and young Peter. They're learning that health is a personal responsibility

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:45.080
and they're doing something about it.

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:59.060
And healthy kids like that young Dickey, they
guarantee this old world a new lease on life.

00:23:02.540 --> 00:23:09.660
Well, I guess I better be getting along now.
Giddy up, Jim, go on boy.

